Subject: a layout reached through the fallback is not the look's own layout

patches/plasma-desktop/0003 moved the default layout out of the Raye
look-and-feel package and into the shell package's contents/layout.js,
which libplasma's Plasma/Shell structure maps to `defaultlayout`. It is
installed, it is byte-identical in the .deb and in the image, and run by
hand it builds the Raye taskbar -- and a new account still got upstream's
bar: kickoff, pager, icontasks, systemtray, digitalclock, showdesktop.
The script was never read.

ShellCorona::loadDefaultLayout() asks the active look-and-feel package for
a layout first and this shell package's `defaultlayout` only if that came
back empty -- in outline:

    script = m_lookAndFeelPackage.filePath("layouts", shell() + "-layout.js");
    if (script.isEmpty()) {
        script = kPackage().filePath("defaultlayout");
    }

It never came back empty. shell/packageplugins/lookandfeel gives every
look-and-feel package whose id is not org.kde.breeze.desktop a KPackage
*fallback* to org.kde.breeze.desktop, and KPackage::Package::filePath()
answers out of that fallback when the active package has no such file of
its own. All three Breeze packages ship
layouts/org.kde.plasma.desktop-layout.js. So a look that carries no layout
-- which is exactly what 0003 made the Raye look -- was handed Breeze's,
and the shell package's own layout below was unreachable for every account
on the machine.

🚨 Measured, not inferred, and on two separate benches -- which matters,
because each one alone has an innocent reading:

  * in the image: moving Breeze's layouts/ aside brings the Raye taskbar
    back, and putting it back brings upstream's. That proves the causation
    and names no line of code.
  * against a real KPackage: a valid look-and-feel package with no layouts/
    and a fallback to org.kde.breeze.desktop is answered Breeze's path by
    filePath(). That names the line.

🚨 And the package split is what says this belongs in plasma-workspace and
not in plasma-desktop, measured in the image's dpkg lists: Breeze's
layouts/ is in plasma-workspace-data, while the Raye layout.js that was
being shadowed is in plasma-desktop-data. The file is one source package
and the thing hiding it is another.

The lookup now asks for a layout the active package really ships. A look
that carries one keeps it -- a third-party theme loses nothing -- and a
look that carries none lets the desktop's own layout through, which is
the whole point of 0003.

The pre-layout lookup one block above has the same flaw and is corrected
with it: Breeze happens to ship no <shell>-prelayout.js today, so nothing
is wrong in the image, but the question being asked is the wrong one and
the next Breeze release would answer it.

⚠️ The emptiness of path() is tested separately, and it is load-bearing:
QString::startsWith(QString()) is true for every string, and an invalid
look-and-feel package has an empty path() while filePath() still answers
out of the fallback. Measured: with that test removed, Breeze's layout
comes through again on that path alone.

⚠️ The other filePath() calls on the look-and-feel package are left as
they are, and that is deliberate. For "defaults" and "plasmoidsetupscripts"
the fallback is the feature: a look that overrides nothing should be given
Breeze's. "layouts" is the one key where falling back preempts a file the
shell package owns.

This is the first patch of the plasma-workspace series, so it also brings
what makes a Raye test run at all. This packaging has the same traps as
plasma-desktop's: it configures with -DBUILD_TESTING=OFF and empties
dh_auto_test, so each of these is written out rather than assumed:

  * Raye's tests live in a directory of their own, gated on RAYE_TESTING,
    which the packaging turns on. Upstream's stay off exactly as Ubuntu
    left them.
  * enable_testing() is called by hand, because include(CTest) only calls
    it when BUILD_TESTING is on; without it add_test() registers nothing.
  * ctest runs with --no-tests=error, because it exits 0 when it finds
    nothing to run.
  * dh calls an override even under nocheck, so that skip is written out.

The test builds the lookup against a real KPackage and three
look-and-feel packages it lays down itself, so it does not read whatever
looks happen to be installed on the build machine. One of its cases
asserts that KPackage still falls a missing file through to the fallback
at all: the day it stops, this patch is no longer needed, and the test
says so rather than leaving a correction nobody can explain.

Reason: this cannot be done outside the source. The order of the two
lookups, and the fallback that makes the first one answer, are both
compiled into plasmashell and its look-and-feel package-structure plugin
-- no configuration file, script or look-and-feel package can reach
either. The alternatives all give up something that was the point of
0003: shipping layouts/ in the Raye look puts the shape of the desktop
back inside a theme, where it travels with the colours; shipping a file
over plasma-desktop's contents/layout.js from another package is a file
conflict; and unsetting LookAndFeelPackage so no look is active would
take Night and Day away. The test has to run where the code is built.

--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -86,6 +86,18 @@
     find_package(Qt6 REQUIRED CONFIG COMPONENTS WaylandCompositor)
 endif()
 
+# Raye's own tests. The packaging configures with BUILD_TESTING=OFF and empties
+# dh_auto_test, so upstream's stay off exactly as Ubuntu left them and Raye's
+# get a switch of their own that the packaging turns on.
+#
+# 🚨 enable_testing() is called by hand here: include(CTest) only calls it when
+# BUILD_TESTING is on. Without it add_test() registers nothing, ctest then
+# reports that it found no test -- and exits 0. That is a build that looks
+# tested and is not.
+if(RAYE_TESTING)
+    enable_testing()
+endif()
+
